  "linked_requirement": "REQ-LUMEN-006",
  "linked_research": "RES-LUMEN-001",
  "spec_file_path": "specs/SPEC-LUMEN-006-PATTERN.json",
  "reasoning": "Establishes graph-theoretical trajectory modeling, Tarjan SCC cycle detection, and mathematical quality indicators. CRIT-LUMEN-072 was rewritten (same id) to bind it to the real compute_recovery_index function/formula (crates/lumen-pattern/src/lib.rs:141-166) instead of untestable prose; the formula was already correctly implemented, only the api_surface entry and criterion wording were missing. CRIT-LUMEN-155's status as of 2026-08-19 was known-failing (red): calculate_monotonicity produced a strictly linear graph with no back-edges, so detect_circular_loops could never fire regardless of node content. STATUS UPDATE, 2026-08-20 (audit-confirmed via direct code read, crates/lumen-pattern/src/lib.rs:37-42,62-86): this has since been fixed -- TrajectoryGraph now carries a target_last_seen map that closes a back-edge whenever a target (target_symbol falling back to target_file) is revisited, giving Tarjan SCC an actual cycle to find. CRIT-LUMEN-155 should be treated as implemented, not red, going forward. Real-data research pass, 2026-08-20: OpenHands' real stuck-detector (a mature, 84k-star agent framework's production loop-detection code, not an analogy) uses five fixed pattern checks (identical action+observation repeat, error-streak-with-a-one-shot-corrective-nudge, agent monologue with no user interruption, period-2 A-B-A-B alternation specifically, and a context-window-error loop OpenHands itself marks as an unsolved TODO) rather than general graph-cycle detection. This confirms Lumen's Tarjan-SCC approach is directionally sound and, in one real dimension, MORE general (arbitrary-length cycles vs. OpenHands' fixed patterns) -- but OpenHands covers two failure categories Tarjan-SCC has no equivalent of: agent monologue (no user interruption for N consecutive turns) and live mid-loop intervention (a corrective nudge, not just post-hoc detection). No criteria are added for these here -- flagged as a possible follow-up spec addition if the user wants 