621	  <p><strong>The distinction that matters most:</strong> three SPEC-002 criteria are drifted not because the code is wrong, but because the test cannot tell wrong from right. Codex's cumulative-token test (<code>CRIT-LUMEN-110</code>) uses exactly two <em>ascending</em> readings — <code>max()</code> would pass identically to the correct last-write logic; it was never exercised with a third, descending line, the one input that would discriminate. The audit-added default arm for unrecognized item types (<code>CRIT-LUMEN-109</code>) and the empty-snapshot no-op guard (<code>CRIT-LUMEN-024</code>) are both real and correct, and both are things you could delete right now without failing anything.</p>
622	
623	  <p>Six SPEC-002 criteria are uncovered or drifted for a different, expected reason: OpenCode's real SQLite rewrite, the 50MB ingestion benchmark, and Qwen fingerprinting were all deliberately deferred this session — not surprises.</p>
624	
625	  <p style="color:var(--ink-faint); font-size:14px;"><code>cargo-llvm-cov</code> is installed but a line-coverage run wasn't part of this pass — the drift-check's per-criterion probing is a stronger signal than a percentage, but a numeric baseline is available on request.</p>
626	</section>
